VOICE = {
    "fen_walk_spine": (
        "I'm Pier-Warden Hask. You'll be off Gael's boat, then. He said he'd start running the crossing again and I didn't believe him.\n\nThere's one road on this isle. It runs from this pier up the spine to the manor gate, and that's it. There are no branches worth the name and nowhere to go but along it.\n\nI haven't walked it since the crossing stopped. That's years now, and I've had every day of those years to do it, and what I've done instead is keep this pier tidy for a boat that wasn't coming.\n\nWalk it to the manor gate and tell me what's changed. I'd rather hear it from somebody who never saw it before. You'll notice things I'd have explained away.",
        None,
        "Hask listens the whole way through without once saying that was always like that.\n\nRight, then, that's a good deal worse than the version I'd settled on. I've been keeping a pier in order on an island that's been getting on with something without me.",
    ),
    "fen_shore_crawlers": (
        "Before anything else, I'd like to be able to walk my own strand.\n\nThe crawlers come up for what the lake delivers, and the lake delivers a great deal here. That isn't new. I've shared this shore with crawlers my whole posting.\n\nWhat's new is the wolves. They follow the crawlers in, and they're rabid, properly rabid, foaming and blind with it, and between the two of them I've spent the last year going from the pier to the hut with a knife in my hand over about forty paces.\n\nClear me a path. That's the entire ambition. I'm not asking for the island or the manor, just forty paces of my own shore.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "fen_cursed_shore": (
        "I'm Elder Gravemoss. Come in past the stones. No, properly past them, don't stand in the gap.\n\nThis is what's left of Bloodmoon Village. There were sixty of us before the curse, and there's what you can see now.\n\nThe worgen stalkers come closer every night. I've measured it with stones. I put a stone where the nearest track is each morning, and the line of stones goes from the treeline to about nine paces from this wall.\n\nAnd the cursed villagers have started looking in the windows. They're our own, people I knew, standing at the glass at night, not trying the door.\n\nI don't know which of those frightens me more. Push them both back off this village.",
        "Go round the village stones and the treeline beyond. Take both, the stalkers and our own.",
        "He puts a fresh stone down, well out past the others.\n\nThat's the line tonight, the furthest it's been since I started counting.\n\nI'll keep putting them down. It's the only measurement anybody on this island has got.",
    ),
    "fen_isle_banshees": (
        "You'll have heard the strand at night by now. Everyone asks within a day.\n\nThe drowned sing. That I can almost live with. It's a thin, far-off thing, and you can tell yourself it's the water in the rocks if you're tired enough.\n\nThe banshees sing back. You can't tell yourself anything about that, because it's a voice answering a voice, and once you've heard the answer you can't go back to hearing the first one as water.\n\nQuiet the banshees for me, and leave the drowned alone. The drowned have as much right to the strand as anyone left here.\n\nI'd like one night where nothing on this island is in conversation with anything else.",
        "Go along the strand after dark. Take the banshees, and leave the drowned be.",
        None,
    ),
    "fen_ancient_curse_beast": (
        "There's one more, and it's older than the curse.\n\nI want to be precise about that, because everything else on this island dates from the same night and this doesn't. My grandmother hunted around it. Her grandmother knew it was there.\n\nIt's a bear the size of a barn, wrapped in its own rot, walking the Cursed Shore since before anybody wrote anything down about this place.\n\nThe curse didn't make it. The curse just stopped it dying, which is a far worse thought, and one I've had a long time to sit with.\n\nNobody who hunts it goes twice. Some of the rabid ones run with it now, so take those off it first.",
        None,
        "Gravemoss sits down on the village stones.\n\nThen the oldest thing on Fenris is gone, and the island is only as old as its worst night.\n\nI'd thought I'd be glad. I find I'm something else, and I haven't got the word for it yet.",
    ),
    "fen_drowned_docks": (
        "I'm Huntsman Corwell. Sit if you like, though nobody does.\n\nThe south-west docks are where the drowned come ashore. They don't come up on the strand. They come to the docks, every time, as if the arrangement still held and that's where you come in.\n\nThe bilge rats come up with them in a mass. You'll hear that before you see it.\n\nI lost a boat down there. I also lost a nephew, and I'm going to say both in the same breath, because that's how the island says it and I'm tired of correcting people.\n\nClear the docks. His name was Tam, he was seventeen, and he wasn't on the boat. Nobody ever asks about that.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "fen_harpoons": (
        "There's something I want off them, and it's a family matter.\n\nThe Drowned Harpooners carry iron heads my family forged. Our mark's on the collar of every one. Most of them are my father's work, and some of them are mine.\n\nAnd every single one is bent the same way, the same bend in the same place. Iron doesn't bend like that when it meets a fish or a rock or a hull. It bends like that when a great many of them hit the same thing at the same angle.\n\nI'd like them back for the smithy, melted down and made right.\n\nAnd I'd like to stop seeing my father's mark on something that's bent like that. That's the real reason, and you may as well have it straight.",
        None,
        "He lays them in a row on the anvil, all bent the same way, and looks at them a long time.\n\nIt's the same angle on every one.\n\nI'll melt them tonight. Whatever they all hit, I'd rather it wasn't still written in my family's iron.",
    ),
    "fen_wretches": (
        "The ones that come up the beach at night were fishermen.\n\nI won't soften that, and I won't have anybody else soften it to me either. They were the Fenris boats' crews, men I went out with, men who taught me the water off the north head.\n\nThey drag themselves up out of the shallows and go a little way up the sand, and then they stop, and they lie there until the tide comes back for them, every night.\n\nThey don't come at the village. They've never come at the village.\n\nSee them off anyway. The alternative is that this goes on for another twenty years, and the last people who remember them as men die of old age watching them do it.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "fen_logbook_page": (
        "It's a single page from a ship's log, pulled from a drowned sailor's coat and still wet through after all this time.\n\nMost of it has run. What's left is the last entry, in a hand that was pressing hard enough to score the paper:\n\nThe Captain locked the hatch from the outside.\n\nThat's where it ends. There's no date under it, no signature, and nothing on the reverse.\n\nThe huntsman in Fenris village keeps the drowned ship's roster in his house, and has done for years.",
        None,
        "Corwell reads it, gets the roster down, and doesn't open it.\n\nWe've had the roster twenty years, and we've told ourselves for twenty years that the hatch jammed.\n\nEverybody on this island knows somebody on that list. And I've just been handed the sentence that means they didn't drown the way we've all agreed they drowned.\n\nHe sets the page on top of the roster.\n\nI'm going to have to tell the village, though not tonight.",
    ),
    "fen_captains_ghost": (
        "I told the village. You should know that before you go, because it changes what I'm asking for.\n\nThe Captain still walks those docks, wrapped in water and furious, and he's hunting the men he locked below. He goes along the line of them every night, looking, and they go into the water to get away from him.\n\nThat's been going on for twenty years.\n\nI had the roster the whole time. I could have opened it any day and counted who was below and who wasn't, and I didn't, because the island had agreed on a story and the story was kinder to everybody, including me.\n\nPut an end to him. And when it's done I'll read the names out properly, all of them, in the right order, and this place can have the true version.",
        None,
        "Corwell has the roster open on the dock post, with the whole village behind him.\n\nRight, the true version, then.\n\nHe begins to read the names.\n\nStay for it if you've got the time. There are a few of these nobody's said out loud since the night it happened.",
    ),
    "fen_curs_grave": (
        "I'm Gravehunter Selke. Mind where you tread. The ground east of here is mostly lids.\n\nThe whole east of this isle is a boneyard for hounds. It was the Baron's hunt. He bred them, he ran them, and when a man of the house died his dogs went in with him. There are hundreds of them, over generations, in proper plots with proper stones.\n\nThey've started digging themselves out. The worgs come up first and the curs follow, and they come up through the lids rather than round them. Think about what that says about the soil here.\n\nThin them out. And watch your footing, because half of what you'll be standing on is a door.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "fen_collars": (
        "The packmasters wear the Baron's collars, and that's what I want off them.\n\nThey're iron, studded, stamped with the Ashbury crest, and properly made by a proper smith, because the Barons spent more on their dogs' fittings than on their tenants' roofs, and everybody on this island knows it.\n\nBring them to me. I'm not collecting. I want them in my hand so I can be certain of where they are, and then I'm going to drop the lot off the north head into deep water.\n\nSomebody will come to this island eventually and find a crate of collars with a noble crest on them and decide there's a hunt to be revived. I'd like there to be nothing for them to find.",
        None,
        "That's the same crest on every one. The Baron owned everything on this isle, even the dogs.",
    ),
    "fen_cur_wardens": (
        "The Cur-Wardens are the part of this I can't explain, and I've stopped pretending I can.\n\nThey keep the graves. They keep the dogs in them. That's what they're for, and it's why the plots held as long as they did, and why the ones they still watch are quiet.\n\nAnd they speak, in a language nobody on this island has heard since the night of the curse. I've asked everyone who might know, including two people who'd have lied to me rather than admit ignorance.\n\nThey're failing now. The graves they watch are opening anyway.\n\nQuiet them for me. I'd rather the plots were unguarded and honest than guarded by something that's lost the thread of what it's doing.",
        None,
        "It's quiet, at last. I don't need to understand what they said.",
    ),
    "fen_alpha_cur": (
        "They follow one. Everything down there follows one.\n\nIt's scarred, grey, and bigger than a cur has any business getting. That's the Baron's last hunt-leader, the dog that was running the pack the night the curse took this island.\n\nIt went into the ground with its master like all the rest. The difference is it came back up remembering the job.\n\nSo you aren't walking at a mad dog. You're walking at a dog that still knows what it was for, which on this island means bringing down whatever the horn points at. There's no horn any more. It's been deciding on its own for twenty years.\n\nTake it off my boneyard.",
        "It's on the boneyard, to the east. It's grey and scarred, and it still remembers the job.",
        "Selke stands looking over the plots.\n\nIt's quiet, properly quiet, for the first time since I took this post.\n\nI'll start closing the lids that are still open. It's years of work, and it's work I can finally do.",
    ),
    "fen_manor_approach": (
        "I'm Scout Merrin. Keep behind the hedge line and keep your voice down, for my sake, not theirs. I've got used to quiet.\n\nI've watched Ashbury's manor for three nights. In three nights I haven't seen one living thing. There's no bird on that roof, no rat in that forecourt. Nothing crosses the lawn, nothing nests in the gutters, and there's a stand of trees on the north side where nothing sings in the morning.\n\nEverything else on this island is crawling. The manor is empty in a way that takes effort.\n\nWalk up to the gate and into the forecourt and come back. I need somebody to have stood in it, because at this distance I've started filling the gaps in myself, and that isn't scouting.",
        None,
        "Nothing stopped you, and that's what worries me.",
    ),
    "fen_manor_guards": (
        "Now you've stood in it, you'll have seen the guard.\n\nThey're Ashbury's own. The footmen walk the forecourt on a circuit and the guardians hold the gate, and they do it properly, at the correct intervals, with correct relief points, and nobody has come to relieve them in twenty years.\n\nThat's what I meant about the manor being empty on purpose. It isn't abandoned, it's staffed.\n\nI've watched a footman walk the same circuit three nights running and stop at the same flagstone each time to look up at a window where nothing has moved since before I was born.\n\nRelieve them for me. That's the word I want used for it, and I'd be glad if you'd use it too.",
        None,
        "So they're relieved. The Baron will be lonely.",
    ),
    "fen_manor_hounds": (
        "There's a thing on the grounds I need you to know before you go in among them.\n\nThe Baron's hounds roam loose and the worgen keepers move with them, and anybody watching from here would say the keepers are handling the pack.\n\nI've seen a keeper eat what it was carrying to feed them. It wasn't in a frenzy. It stopped on the path, in the open, ate what was in the pail, and then went on to the kennels with an empty pail and stood there a while.\n\nSo whatever hierarchy that household had, it isn't the one on paper, and the hounds have been hungry for a long time. Clear the grounds, the hounds and the keepers both.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "fen_baron_ashbury": (
        "And the Baron himself, in the hall, at the end of it.\n\nHe ruled this island alive and he rules it worse dead. Everything you've cleared out there, the footmen on their circuit, the hounds, the keepers, the nobles with their rings on, is still arranged around him. He's the reason the manor is staffed.\n\nHe sits in the hall and waits for the next guest to disappoint him. I've watched through the long window. He's sitting the way a man sits when he expects somebody to be shown in.\n\nI'd very much like him disappointed. And then I'd like this island to stop being a household and go back to being a place.",
        None,
        "Merrin comes out from behind the hedge line for the first time.\n\nThe forecourt's empty, the whole thing. There's no circuit, no relief points, nothing on the lawn.\n\nThere's a bird on the roof. There's actually a bird on that roof. Three nights I watched, and nothing would land on it.",
    ),
    "fen_altar_arch": (
        "I'm Warden Orlan. Ten years I've watched that arch, and I'll tell you exactly what I've got for it, which is nothing new.\n\nThe curse was cast under the west ridge, in the Shadowpine Altar, and the arch at the top of the stair is where it came out. That much everyone on this island agrees on.\n\nWhat I need is somebody standing at the foot of that stair telling me the air is still wrong.\n\nI'll be honest with you. I've stood there so many times that I can no longer tell whether it's wrong or whether I simply expect it to be. That's what ten years at one post does, and nobody warns you about it.\n\nGo down and stand at the bottom. Then tell me.",
        None,
        "So it's still wrong. Of course it is. Thank you for confirming.",
    ),
    "fen_altar_cultists": (
        "The Shadowpine Circle never left.\n\nThat's the part that takes people a while. The curse was cast, it worked, the island went the way it went, and the cult that did it stayed, and kept the rites going, and has kept them going ever since.\n\nThere's no cause left. Whatever they wanted, they got it or they didn't, and either way it was settled before any of us were born.\n\nThey're still down there doing the daily work of it. There are cultists on the stair, bone-warders risen to help, and the whole apparatus turning over like a mill with no grain in it.\n\nThin them out. I've got no better word for what I'm asking, and no better reason than that it ought to stop.",
        None,
        "The rites will go on, I suppose, but more slowly.",
    ),
    "fen_altar_aldric": (
        "West of the stair is the Bone Circle, and Aldric keeps it, and he's kept it for centuries. I've checked that against three sources that don't agree about anything else.\n\nHere's what you need to understand before you go west. He's made of the things he keeps. He isn't armoured in them, he's made of them, and the Circle and the Bonewarden aren't two things that happen to be in the same chamber.\n\nThat means the Circle gets smaller when he does. I'd rather you knew that going in than worked it out in the middle.\n\nGo and unmake him.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "fen_altar_fenris": (
        "The top of the Altar Ring is where this ends.\n\nAlpha Fenris is the first worgen. I don't mean the first on this island. I mean the first, the one the curse made before it had been made twice, and the one this isle took its name from.\n\nEverything you've fought on Fenris descends from what's standing at the top of that ring: the pack at Bloodmoon, the keepers at the manor, the Moonhowl below him.\n\nI've waited ten years at the bottom of that stair to be able to ask somebody this. Go up and finish it. And then I'd like to go home, which is a sentence I haven't let myself say out loud since I took the post.",
        None,
        "Orlan doesn't look at the arch.\n\nIt's quiet, and the air's just air.\n\nThat's ten years. I'd built the whole of myself around standing at the bottom of a stair, and now there's a stair and nothing at the top of it, and I've no idea who I'll be tomorrow.\n\nThat isn't your problem, and it isn't ungratefulness. Thank you, truly.",
    ),
    "fen_isle_reckoning": (
        "Come to the well, and stand there a moment while I say this properly.\n\nFour reports have reached me since you came off that boat. The Curse-Beast is down, the oldest thing on this island. The Captain is down, and Corwell read the true roster out on the dock in front of everyone. The Alpha Cur is off the boneyard. The Baron is out of his hall.\n\nI've lived on Fenris my whole life, and I've never known four of those to be true at once. I'd have told you it wasn't possible, because this island doesn't get better, it only gets later.\n\nSo thank you, from Bloodmoon, and from a village that's down to its last few people and has had nothing to say thank you for in twenty years.",
        None,
        "He puts a stone down on the far side of the well, well past the line he's been marking.\n\nThat's where the nearest track was this morning, right out at the treeline, where it was when I was a boy.\n\nI'll keep putting them down. But I think I'll be putting them down for a different reason from now on.",
    ),
}


